# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Their Own Words v2 content — Acts III & IV. See _tow_content_a.py for the
entry format. Sources: ('cand', N) -> 03-data/learn-quote-candidates.json;
('gap', key, N) -> 03-data/learn-quote-gaps.json."""

ACTS_3_4 = [

# ═══════════════ ACT III — "The Long Middle" ═══════════════

dict(qid='a3-camp-1', act=3, theme='camp', source=('cand', 27), recip='to his mother', date_str='September 18, 1863',
  stem='Alexander’s whole corps was marched past the coffins after an execution. Why did the army stage it that way?',
  choices=['Regulations required every soldier to witness burials', 'As deliberate deterrence — desertion had become an epidemic', 'To honor the executed men’s prior service', 'To let surgeons certify the deaths publicly'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. “That we might see them and learn a lesson” — Alexander understood the choreography perfectly, and even approved of it.",
  whyWrong={'0': "No such regulation — ordinary burials happened constantly and drew no formations. This procession was designed, not required.",
            '2': "The opposite of honor — deserters were shot seated on their own coffins, an old and deliberate disgrace."},
  expansion="Roughly two hundred thousand Union soldiers deserted during the war, and 1863 — after Fredericksburg's futile slaughter, the miserable Mud March, and the first conscription — was the crisis year, with absences running toward one man in ten. The army's answer was theater as much as law: of some 267 executions all war, most were for desertion, staged before formed divisions with bands playing the dead march, the condemned seated on their coffins, and the troops filed past afterward at close range. Officers debated whether it worked; Lincoln pardoned so many 'leg cases' (his phrase for men whose legs ran away with them) that his generals despaired. What the spectacle certainly did was mark a change in the war itself — the volunteer crusade of 1861 had become an institution that killed its own to hold the line. Every man marched past those coffins did the same moral arithmetic Alexander does on paper here.",
  context="Read Alexander's verdict twice: 'a very salutary effect upon the army.' No horror, no protest — approval. This is the boy who joked about hen roosts fifteen months ago, now two years a soldier, writing home from the Rapidan line like a small magistrate. The hardening isn't a flaw in him; it's the war's signature on him, and the letters let you watch it form in real time — which is precisely what a diary of institutional violence looks like from inside. Worth noticing too WHO he tells: his mother. By 1863 he has stopped fully sorting the war into 'fit for home' and 'not' — the categories themselves are wearing out. His next dated letters march toward Chattanooga, where the 60th New York will climb Lookout Mountain under fire in November."),

dict(qid='a3-body-1', act=3, theme='body', source=('cand', 24), recip='to his mother', date_str='July 4, 1863',
  stem='Alexander wrote this sentence on July 4, 1863, near a Pennsylvania town. What is he describing the morning after?',
  choices=['The Battle of Gettysburg', 'The fall of Vicksburg', 'The Battle of Chancellorsville', 'The draft riots in New York City'],
  answer=0,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. His 60th New York had held Culp's Hill; on Independence Day the armies buried more than seven thousand dead where they lay.",
  whyWrong={'1': "Vicksburg surrendered that same day — the war's great double blow — but a thousand miles west on the Mississippi. Alexander is standing in Pennsylvania.",
            '3': "The riots come nine days later — set off in part by the casualty lists this very burial detail was creating."},
  expansion="Nine words, and the detail that swallows the rest: 'our AND the rebel's dead.' Gettysburg left more than seven thousand corpses and several thousand horse carcasses on the fields of a town of 2,400 people, in July heat; burial details worked through the smell for days, and rain kept unearthing the shallow work all summer. The crisis is directly why Pennsylvania chartered a Soldiers' National Cemetery on the ground that fall, reinterring the Union dead by state — and why, at its dedication on November 19, Lincoln stood up after the two-hour oration and reframed the war in 272 words. Alexander's burial detail and the Gettysburg Address are two ends of one logistical problem: what a democracy owes the bodies its arguments produce. His regiment had defended Culp's Hill — trenches his brigade commander, George Sears Greene, had insisted on digging when digging was still considered timid. The spades saved the hill, and probably Alexander.",
  context="This is the collection's shortest great sentence, and its restraint is the content: no adjectives, no theology, just the work of the morning reported to his mother in the same breath as the weather. Alexander wrote it exhausted — the 60th had fought on Culp's Hill through the battle's second and third days — and the fuller letter (open it from here) puts his survival in God's hands with an addendum his family would have read on their knees. Note the date one more time: July 4, 1863, Gettysburg won, Vicksburg fallen, and a farm boy from Champlain burying Confederates in Pennsylvania clay. If a single question in this game holds the war entire — scale, grief, duty, and the strange administrative ordinariness of catastrophe — it's this one. Follow the map spoke: the site's timeline knows exactly where he stood."),

dict(qid='a3-purse-1', act=3, theme='purse', source=('cand', 42), recip='to his mother', date_str='July 20, 1863',
  stem='Charles says two hometown men were “lucky to escape” — for $300. Escape what?',
  choices=['A Confederate raid on their county', 'The 1863 federal draft, by paying the commutation fee', 'Arrest for smuggling goods to Canada', 'A lawsuit over unpaid enlistment bounties'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. The Enrollment Act let a drafted man pay $300 — commutation — instead of serving. Roughly a year's wages for a laborer: Charles's “doesn't grow on every bush” is the whole class politics of the clause in one phrase.",
  whyWrong={'0': "No raid touched Champlain in 1863 — the escape was from a government envelope, not from cavalry.",
            '3': "Bounty disputes were real, but $300 was the draft law's own printed number — the most famous price in America that July."},
  expansion="The Enrollment Act of March 1863 made every male citizen (and immigrant who'd declared intent) aged 20–45 liable to conscription — and then sold two exits: hire a substitute, or pay $300 commutation. Congress meant the fee as a price CEILING to stop substitute costs from spiraling, and economically it worked; politically it was a catastrophe of optics, a year's wages posted as the price of a body. Days before Charles wrote, that number had helped burn New York: the draft riots of July 13–16 killed at least 119 people, lynched Black New Yorkers, and torched the Colored Orphan Asylum — the bloodiest civil disorder in American history, put down partly by regiments marched straight from Gettysburg. Congress repealed commutation in 1864 (substitution stayed — the wealthier exit survived). For the record's sake: about 8% of Union soldiers were draftees or substitutes; the shame campaign, the bounties, and men like the Hubbells did the rest.",
  context="'Sid and Silas' are hometown boys — one of them, Silas, is where Charles mails his own absentee ballot in Act IV, so the tone here is teasing family-adjacent gossip, not the acid Alexander pours on the substitute-hoarders. But don't miss the ledger under the joke: Charles is a private on $13 a month; $300 is nearly two YEARS of his pay; and he'd enlisted voluntarily in the awful autumn of 1862 while Sid and Silas waited for the lottery. 'Lucky to escape' carries no bitterness on its surface — Charles doesn't do bitterness — and all the arithmetic of it underneath. That doubleness is Charles's whole register, and it's why historians prize voices like his: the era's fairness debates, priced to the dollar, in a letter that still remembers to be kind."),

dict(qid='a3-nation-1', act=3, theme='nation', source=('cand', 43), recip='to his mother', date_str='July 20, 1863',
  stem='A week after deadly anti-draft riots in New York, Charles reports the army’s mood. Why did soldiers LIKE the draft?',
  choices=['It meant their own enlistments would end sooner', 'It felt like justice — the men at home would finally share the burden', 'Drafted men were paid more, raising everyone’s wages', 'They expected the draft to end the war within weeks'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. To men in the ranks, conscription wasn't tyranny — it was the stay-at-homes' turn. Charles says it without a flicker of sympathy for the rioters.",
  whyWrong={'0': "Enlistments ran their full term regardless — the draft added men beside them; it released no one.",
            '3': "Nobody at the front thought the war was weeks from over — Gettysburg's wagons of wounded were still moving north as he wrote."},
  expansion="The same law produced riots in Manhattan and satisfaction in the camps — and that split is the lesson. Soldiers' letters across every Union army that July say what Charles says, often word for word: the draft pleased the men. Three years of watching 'fireside patriots' cheer from armchairs (Act II taught you the phrase) had built a deep account of resentment, and conscription read as the government finally collecting the debt. Some regiments offered to be marched to New York to 'enforce the draft' personally; several were actually sent, fresh from Gettysburg, and did it. Primary sources disagreeing is not a problem to resolve — it's the shape of the truth: 'northern opinion' on conscription was not one thing, and every position had a return address. When you read that the draft was hated, ask: by whom, in which street, and who was already in uniform when they said so.",
  context="Same letter as the $300 question — July 20, 1863, Alexandria — and the pairing is deliberate: one page of Charles holds both the class complaint (commutation's price) and the soldiers' cheer (the draft as justice), which tells you these weren't opposing camps but one mind holding two true things. Charles had watched the war empty his family's house: Henry dead ten months, Alexander at Gettysburg (word of his survival was still in the mail when this was written), James in uniform, himself guarding Alexandria's railroads. From inside that arithmetic, 'nothing has pleased the soldiers so for a long time' isn't cruelty toward Manhattan's dead — it's the settled opinion of a man whose family had paid retail while others negotiated. History rarely hands you a cleaner specimen of standpoint shaping judgment."),

dict(qid='a3-family-1', act=3, theme='family', source=('cand', 61), recip='to Alexander', date_str='November 15, 1863',
  stem='Frances lists the fates her family imagined for Alexander during a silence in his letters. What does her list reveal about the home front?',
  choices=['Families followed the war closely enough to know its horrors by name', 'Northern newspapers were censored into vagueness', 'Families of soldiers were kept informed by the War Department', 'Civilians knew little about conditions in the armies'],
  answer=0,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Libby Prison, field hospitals, the “unknown” dead — she names the war's specific machinery of loss, because a year earlier it had taken Henry. Home fronts were not innocent; they were informed and helpless at once.",
  whyWrong={'1': "The papers were graphic and often wrong — she complains elsewhere that there's 'not the least reliance to be placed upon' them. Too much information, not too little.",
            '2': "No such service existed — which is exactly why her imagination had to do the notifying."},
  expansion="Every silence between letters opened the same catalog. Libby Prison — a converted Richmond tobacco warehouse holding Union officers — was infamous enough by late 1863 to furnish nightmares in a kitchen on the Canadian border; Belle Isle and, the next year, Andersonville would darken the catalog further. Note the mechanism: Frances isn't uninformed, she is OVER-informed by a press that printed prison exposés, hospital sketches, and error-riddled casualty lists at industrial speed, leaving families to run probabilities on their own sons. Historians who write about the Civil War home front now lean hard on letters like this one, because they record what the war's information economy actually did to the people holding the envelopes: it converted love into surveillance — of mails, of papers, of rumor — with no power to act on any of it. 'Anxiety' is our word; her word, in another letter, was 'trembling.'",
  context="Count the imagined fates: prison, mangling, the unknown dead — and then the phrase that gives away the family's interior weather, 'our blessed lamented Henry.' Fourteen months after Antietam, Henry's death has become part of the household vocabulary, the fixed star her other fears navigate by. The occasion for all this was mundane: Alexander's regiment had been transferred WEST that fall — Virginia to Tennessee by rail, eleven hundred miles, one of the war's great logistical feats — and the mails simply lagged the movement. He was fine; he was marching toward Lookout Mountain, where in twelve days he'd be wounded (the family's luck holding by inches again). The letters crossing in the mail, her terror and his reassurance passing each other somewhere in Ohio — that's the home front's war, in its purest form."),

dict(qid='a3-camp-2', act=3, theme='camp', source=('gap', 'III-camp', 0), recip='to his mother', date_str='November 21, 1863',
  stem='Ten-minute alarm, knapsacks packed, march to headquarters — “and marched back again.” What soldier’s experience is Alexander recording?',
  choices=['A failed mutiny in his division', 'The army’s constant false alarms and cancelled orders — “hurry up and wait”', 'A punishment detail for his company', 'A night attack that was beaten off'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. The dash in his sentence — “and a — and marched back again” — is a soldier declining to write what he thought of it. Armies ran on orders, counter-orders, and the patience of the men whipsawed between them.",
  whyWrong={'3': "No attack came — that's the joke's engine. The alarm was real enough to pack for and empty enough to march home from.",
            '2': "Punishment details existed, but a whole regiment under arms with knapsacks is an operational alarm, not discipline."},
  expansion="For every hour of battle the Civil War soldier absorbed weeks of this: alarms at midnight, orders to cook three days' rations that meant nothing, columns started and countermanded, the same hill climbed and unclimbed. The phenomenon had structural causes — armies of 60,000 steered by couriers and flag signals, intelligence running on rumor, commanders probing and feinting — but the man in the ranks experienced it as pure institutional whim, and soldier letters made a genre of it. The date matters here: November 21, 1863, outside Chattanooga, was the eve of one of the war's great set-pieces — within four days Alexander's 60th New York would storm up Lookout Mountain in the 'Battle Above the Clouds.' The midnight fall-in he mocks was the sound of that battle assembling itself, invisible from inside. That, too, is the lesson: soldiers lived the war one absurd order at a time; the shape of campaigns belonged to maps they never saw.",
  context="The dash is the best-edited moment in Alexander's whole correspondence — 'we marched over to Division headquarters and a — and marched back again.' You can hear the swallowed profanity; his mother could too, and that was the game between them. Two years of soldiering have given him a connoisseur's palate for military absurdity, and it's worth savoring here because of what the calendar is about to do: four days after this letter, he goes up Lookout Mountain under fire, and in a week he's writing home about a minié ball. The collection holds them side by side — the eye-roll and the wound — which is the truest thing any archive shows about war: the boredom and the terror weren't different wars; they were the same week."),

dict(qid='a3-body-2', act=3, theme='body', source=('cand', 23), recip='to his mother', date_str='July 4, 1863',
  stem='Alexander credits his survival at Gettysburg to “God’s great kindness.” What role did faith play for Civil War soldiers?',
  choices=['Armies were officially secular — religion was discouraged in camp', 'It was the era’s core equipment for facing death — from private fatalism to camp-wide revivals', 'Only chaplains were permitted to hold services', 'Faith declined sharply as the war lengthened'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Victorian America met death with Providence. A man who might be shot at dawn needed a way to stand it, and “God preserved me” was the century's sturdiest sentence — written home after every battle by hundreds of thousands of hands.",
  whyWrong={'0': "The opposite — chaplains were commissioned, tracts shipped by the million, and generals (Howard, Jackson) wore their piety publicly.",
            '3': "The war deepened it, if anything: the great camp revivals of 1863–64 swept both armies, converting tens of thousands in winter quarters."},
  expansion="Civil War soldiers faced the era's worst odds with the era's strongest cosmology. Providence — the conviction that God governed every outcome — let a man march at a stone wall without going mad: if his hour was appointed, dodging was faithless; if it wasn't, the bullets weren't his. Historians reading thousands of these letters find the formula everywhere, ranging from rote to radiant: 'God has again preserved my life' is practically the war's second signature. The institutions kept pace — the U.S. Christian Commission distributed millions of Bibles and tracts, and the winter revivals of 1863–64 in both armies were among the largest religious events in American history. And the same cosmology had to work the other side of the ledger: for families, 'God's will' was the only available sense-making when the letter came back edged in black. Watch Frances's letters in this game do exactly that work.",
  context="Same morning as 'We are burying our and the rebel's dead today' — this is the sentence Alexander wrote FIRST, the frame before the fact. Put them together and you have the period's whole grammar of survival: gratitude to Providence, then the burial detail, no contradiction felt. His piety was not boilerplate; the letters show a young man who attended prayer meetings, refused whiskey rations, and reached for scripture under stress (by 1865, his valediction letter will thank 'the providence of God' for the nation itself). But here's the thing to hold: Henry's death ten months earlier had already tested the family's theology to its foundation — and it held, because it had to. When Alexander tells his mother God preserved him at Gettysburg, he is also, gently, answering the question neither of them writes down: why not at Antietam?"),

dict(qid='a3-purse-2', act=3, theme='purse', source=('cand', 40), recip='to his mother', date_str='February 22, 1863',
  stem='Charles asks for “greenbacks” because “Uncle Sam’s money passes the best here.” What were greenbacks?',
  choices=['Gold coins minted for army pay', 'The first federal paper money — legal tender backed by the government’s word, not gold', 'Confederate currency captured and reused', 'Private banknotes issued by New York banks'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. The Legal Tender Act of 1862 created a national paper currency — green-inked, gold-free, good by law for all debts. A revolution in what money WAS, and Charles is field-testing it for you.",
  whyWrong={'3': "That's exactly the old world greenbacks replaced — a chaos of thousands of private banknotes, discounted differently in every town.",
            '0': "Gold coin vanished into hoards the moment paper appeared (Gresham's law in action) — a private might never see specie at all."},
  expansion="Before 1862 the United States had no national paper currency — money meant gold, silver, or a jungle of private banknotes worth different amounts in different counties. War spending broke that system in a year, and Congress's answer, the Legal Tender Act, was among the war's most radical laws: paper 'greenbacks,' backed by nothing but the government's authority, lawful for every debt. Conservatives called it unconstitutional alchemy; the war made it stick, and with the National Banking Acts of 1863–64 (which taxed private notes out of existence) it permanently nationalized American money. Greenbacks did float against gold — sinking to 39 cents on the gold dollar in the dark summer of 1864, tracking Union fortunes like a poll — which made a soldier's preference for them a small act of fiscal patriotism and practical sense at once: whatever a sutler thought of the war news, the law said he had to take the green.",
  context="Charles the quartermaster's mind at work: one sentence, and it's the most economically literate in the collection. 'Passes the best HERE' — he's comparing circulation quality across currencies in occupied Virginia the way his mother would candle eggs, and he's right: in an army town thick with sutlers, state banknotes from Clinton County were nearly waste paper while federal green passed at par. The Hubbell letters are threaded with this financial traffic — checks, express packages, greenbacks folded into pages, requests like this one — because the family economy ran through the mails. Notice the date, too: Washington's Birthday, 1863, the middle of the war's grimmest winter, and a twenty-year-old private is calmly optimizing the family's currency portfolio. Habits like that are why Charles survives the war as the family's bookkeeper of everything — including, in this act's family question, its grief."),

dict(qid='a3-nation-2', act=3, theme='nation', source=('cand', 25), recip='to his mother', date_str='August 16, 1863',
  stem='Alexander reports citizens “caught at the R.R. Depot trying to run away.” Run away from what?',
  choices=['A yellow fever quarantine', 'The federal draft — men fleeing town before the enrollment could catch them', 'Confederate conscription agents operating in the North', 'Debt collectors after the war tax'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Draft evasion was the Enrollment Act's shadow: skip town before the lottery, slip to Canada, disappear into the next county's anonymity. The depot was the choke point, and the provost guard knew it.",
  whyWrong={'2': "No Confederate agents conscripted in New York — the runners were dodging their OWN government's lottery.",
            '3': "War taxes existed (the first income tax among them), but nobody fled by rail from a 3% levy — the draft was the train-ticket fear."},
  expansion="For every draft rioter, there were many quiet evaders: an estimated 160,000 men simply failed to report when called, and tens of thousands more arranged to be elsewhere when the enrollers came — Canada above all, so famously that 'skedaddling to Canada' entered the language. Enforcement fell to provost marshals with watch lists at depots and border crossings, and to a bounty system for informers; the Champlain corridor where the Hubbells lived, one train ride from the border, was precisely the kind of leak the government tried hardest to plug. Evasion, commutation, substitution, exemption brokers, invented ailments — the Enrollment Act spawned an entire gray economy of avoidance, and the paperwork it left behind is one of historians' best windows into who actually bore the war. The draft's deepest work was never the men it conscripted (a small fraction of the army); it was the volunteering it frightened into existence and the national argument it forced about what citizens owe.",
  context="Alexander reports the depot arrests with the dry relish of a man who has standing to enjoy them — two years in uniform, one brother dead, two more serving. This is the third panel of his class-anger triptych (fireside patriots, substitute-hoarders, and now the runners), and it's the calmest, because by August 1863 the government had finally built the machinery to do his resenting for him. Geography sharpens the point: these are NORTH-country men caught running the same corridor to Canada that the family farm sits on; some of the names, we can assume, were names the Hubbells knew. The letters never gloat by name — that restraint is constant in this family — but the temperature of sentences like this one is its own census of what the town's war had cost the people who answered it."),

dict(qid='a3-family-2', act=3, theme='family', source=('cand', 41), recip='to his mother', date_str='June 10, 1863',
  stem='Nine months after Henry’s death, Charles pushes his mother to “apply at once for Henry’s back pay and bounty.” What is this letter doing?',
  choices=['Settling a quarrel over Henry’s belongings', 'Navigating the government’s machinery of death — the paperwork a family owed and was owed when a soldier died', 'Requesting a pension for Charles himself', 'Trying to have Henry’s body moved home'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. A dead soldier's family had to CLAIM what he was owed — back pay, the unpaid bounty, eventually a pension — through affidavits, witnesses, and months of correspondence. Grief came with forms.",
  whyWrong={'3': "Many families did retrieve bodies — but Henry's was never identified. There was nothing to move: only money, and the paperwork to pry it loose.",
            '2': "Charles was twenty-one and healthy; pensions came for disability or to dependents of the dead. This claim is Henry's — pursued by the family he left."},
  expansion="The Union's promises to its soldiers survived them: back pay to the date of death, the unpaid portion of the $100 bounty, and — under the pension act of July 1862 — support for widows, orphans, and dependent mothers. But nothing arrived automatically. A family had to prove the death (hard, when the body was never identified), prove the relationship, prove dependency for a mother's pension, all by affidavit through a bureaucracy inventing itself under load. An industry of claim agents sprang up, honest and predatory alike, taking fees from the bereaved. What began in letters like this one became, by century's end, the largest social-welfare system in American history — Civil War pensions consumed more than 40% of the federal budget at their peak, the ancestor of every entitlement that followed. The archive holds this family's own thread of that machinery, down to Alexander's pension cards in the National Archives.",
  context="Notice who is managing the family's claim: not the widow Frances — Charles, twenty-one, writing procedure from a camp in Virginia: 'apply AT ONCE,' the italics practically audible. Every family under grief redistributes its competencies, and the Hubbells' redistribution is visible across this archive: Alexander carries the emotional register, James the reticence, and Charles the ledgers — pay, bounties, checks, greenbacks, and now his dead brother's accounts receivable. There's a tenderness in the officiousness, if you read it right: pursuing Henry's back pay was the one thing a brother in Virginia could still DO for him. The claim that starts here runs for years through the family's paper trail. The money mattered — a widow's farm, remember — but the doing mattered as much; bureaucracy, it turns out, was one of the era's forms of mourning."),

# ═══════════════ ACT IV — "The Grind" ═══════════════

dict(qid='a4-camp-1', act=4, theme='camp', source=('cand', 51), recip='to his sister Fannie', date_str='June 21, 1864',
  stem='James — the youngest brother, newly in the field in Louisiana — is proud of surviving a day’s march. What was the ordinary soldier’s main physical work of the war?',
  choices=['Digging fortifications', 'Marching — armies moved on foot, twenty miles and more a day', 'Loading supply wagons', 'Standing guard at night'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. For every hour in battle a soldier spent weeks on the road — and hardening to the march WAS becoming a soldier, in 1861 or 1864 alike.",
  whyWrong={'0': "By 1864 spades mattered enormously — but entrenching was episodic. The march was daily, universal, and what letters complain about most.",
            '3': "Picket duty was constant but stationary misery. What broke new men in was the roads."},
  expansion="A Civil War army was a walking city — 60,000 men, thousands of wagons and animals — and it moved at the speed of feet. Twelve to fifteen miles was a standard day, twenty-five a forced march, and the cost was paid in the body: blisters, sunstroke (men died of it on the road), forty pounds of rifle, ammunition, and knapsack shaping shoulders all summer, and 'straggling' — falling out of the column — as the army's chronic disease. Veterans learned the economies: half the knapsack discarded by June, coffee at every halt, the shuffling route-step that ate miles. The capacity to march was strategy itself — Jackson's 'foot cavalry' in 1862 and Sherman's westerners in 1864 won campaigns with their legs — and the difference between a recruit and a veteran was measured less in courage than in feet that had stopped bleeding. James's tent-floor collapse is every soldier's first tuition payment.",
  context="James is the family's late arrival and its gentlest puzzle: bookish, West Point-schooled but new to campaigning, twenty years old, writing to Fannie with a self-deprecation none of his brothers use — 'considering I had used my legs so little.' The honesty is the charm: no swagger, just a young man reporting his own softness ruefully to his sister. His war accelerates brutally from here: within four months of this letter the 153rd is in the Shenandoah under Sheridan, and at Cedar Creek in October, James — the boy proud of standing one hard march — will be wounded and walk miles on it rather than be captured (this act's other Body question holds that sentence). Read them as a pair and you get the whole veteranizing process in one man, compressed into a single campaign season: June's sore legs, October's iron ones."),

dict(qid='a4-body-1', act=4, theme='body', source=('cand', 29), recip='to his mother', date_str='August 22, 1864',
  stem='Alexander’s diagnosis points at the Civil War’s deadliest enemy. What killed the most soldiers, North and South?',
  choices=['Artillery and rifle fire', 'Disease', 'Prison camps', 'Cavalry raids'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Roughly two of every three deaths were from disease, not combat — and scurvy in a veteran army outside Atlanta tells you the supply system could move bullets faster than vegetables.",
  whyWrong={'0': "That's the war we see in paintings — but the hospitals tell the real story. Battle wounds killed about one soldier for every two that disease took.",
            '2': "The prison camps were lethal — Andersonville above all — but their toll sits far below the armies' own camps and hospitals."},
  expansion="Scurvy is a grocery-list disease — no vitamin C, roughly ninety days, and the body starts unstitching itself: gums, joints, old wounds reopening. That a veteran of the world's best-supplied army got 'genuine scurvy' outside Atlanta is a precise diagnostic of the campaign: a hundred days of continuous operations at the end of a single-track railroad from Chattanooga, every car prioritized for ammunition and hardtack over onions. The armies knew the cure without knowing the chemistry — the Sanitary Commission shipped vegetables by the boatload, 'desiccated vegetables' rode in the wagons, and surgeons prescribed potatoes like medicine — but the Atlanta logistics simply outran the diet. Multiply Alexander's bed by the war: 250,000 Union dead of disease against 110,000 of wounds, typhoid and dysentery the great reapers, scurvy the quiet accomplice that weakened men for everything else. The Civil War's true casualty engine had a supply manifest, not a trigger.",
  context="'I am NOT wounded but am afflicted...' — the sentence's first move is triage for his mother's heart, executed before the diagnosis. It's August 1864; she has Henry two years dead, James in the Shenandoah, Charles in Virginia, and Alexander has already survived Antietam, Gettysburg, and a Lookout Mountain bullet — so of course his opener defuses. Then the wry precision: 'what the surgeon calls GENUINE scurvy,' as if impressed by the authenticity of his own affliction. Within days he's evacuated down the very railroad that starved his diet (the hospital-train letter sits in this archive too), and that same month, in a coincidence he himself laughed at on paper, all three surviving brothers were in hospitals at once in three different states. The Wellness Ledger — one spoke below — tracks every entry of it; this question is that whole surface distilled to one sentence."),

dict(qid='a4-purse-1', act=4, theme='purse', source=('cand', 45), recip='to his mother', date_str='January 3, 1864',
  stem='Charles reports Alexander could collect $1,000 for reenlisting in 1864. Why was the government suddenly offering sums like that?',
  choices=['Inflation had made $1,000 worth little', 'Veteran enlistments were expiring just as the war’s hardest campaigns loomed', 'It was back pay owed from 1861', 'Officers’ commissions were being sold openly'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. The three-year men of 1861 — the army's spine — could all go home in 1864. Federal, state, and town bounties stacked up to four figures to keep them.",
  whyWrong={'0': "Wartime inflation was real — prices roughly doubled over the war — but $1,000 was still a small fortune; that's why it worked.",
            '2': "Back pay arrived chronically late, but never compounded into that — this was new money, for a new signature."},
  expansion="The winter of 1863–64 held a quiet crisis bigger than any battle: the 1861 three-year enlistments — the veteran core of every Union army — expired in mid-1864, precisely when Grant planned the war's climactic campaigns. The government's offer: $402 federal bounty, a thirty-day 'veteran furlough' home, 'Veteran Volunteer' chevrons, and whatever states and towns stacked on top — packages that could clear $1,000, six-plus years of a private's base pay. About 136,000 veterans re-upped, over half of those eligible, and historians still marvel at it: men who knew EXACTLY what Virginia and Georgia would cost signed for more of it. (Peer pressure was engineered — regiments re-enlisting three-quarters strength kept their flags and numbers; going home meant dissolving the unit that had become home.) The bounty system's rot — the professional 'bounty jumper' — rode along as always. But the veteranization of 1864 was arguably the Union's decisive quartermaster's victory: it bought the army that outlasted Lee.",
  context="Brotherly financial advice, edition of January 1864: 'I think Alex had better NOT reenlist although he can get $1,000 bounty.' Read Charles's ledger between the lines — he's weighing a thousand dollars against a brother, and pricing the brother higher. The family had standing to do this math: Henry's bounty had arrived as back-pay-to-the-dead paperwork (you saw Charles managing it in Act III), so no Hubbell romanticized what the government's money could cost to collect. Alexander stayed in regardless — his 60th New York veteranized and he served to the surrender — and it's worth noticing that the archive holds no letter of him agonizing over it; whatever his reasons, they didn't need explaining to his family. The two facts sit side by side, January 1864: the accountant brother saying don't, and the soldier brother staying. Both were forms of love; only one left a paper trail."),

dict(qid='a4-nation-1', act=4, theme='nation', source=('cand', 49), recip='to his mother', date_str='November 4, 1864',
  stem='Charles mailed his ballot home from the field in 1864. What made this election historic?',
  choices=['It was the first U.S. election held during a war', 'Soldiers were furloughed home en masse to vote', 'It pioneered absentee voting for soldiers in the field', 'Lincoln ran unopposed after McClellan withdrew'],
  answer=2,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Nineteen states passed laws letting soldiers vote from the field — the first mass absentee voting in American history. Charles's ballot rode home to Champlain in an envelope.",
  whyWrong={'0': "The election of 1812 (Madison, mid-war) already holds that title — 1864's marvel wasn't that it happened, but HOW: ballots collected in army camps.",
            '1': "Some furloughs happened where field voting wasn't legal — but Charles tells you exactly how HIS ballot traveled: he mailed it to Silas."},
  expansion="No democracy had ever done what the Union did in November 1864: held a full national election in the middle of a civil war, with the army voting from the trenches, and stood ready to hand power to the opposition if it lost. Nineteen states built the machinery — commissioners in camps, proxy ballots home by mail (New York's method, hence Charles's envelope to Silas) — and the soldier vote went about 78% for Lincoln, the men choosing to finish the war over the general who once led them. But averages flatten: Charles's company went TWO for Lincoln. The 153rd New York was raised in Fulton and Montgomery counties, Democratic country, and it voted like its kitchens, not its commander-in-chief. Lincoln called the election's mere occurrence the point: 'we can not have free government without elections.' The fact that a private could disagree with his president by mail, mid-war, unpunished, and say so to his mother — that IS the lesson, and it needed no averaging.",
  context="Here is Charles's political voice at full temperature, which is to say barely above room: 'I sent my vote to Silas, voted for Lincoln. Only two votes for Lincoln in our Co.' No editorial on his comrades, no anxiety at being 2-of-a-company — just the fact, filed. Two weeks earlier the battle of Cedar Creek had rolled straight over this camp (his brother James was wounded and captured-then-freed in it); the regiment voted with the Valley's smoke barely cleared. Set Charles beside Alexander's Copperhead disgust (this act's other Nation question) and you get the family's political spread: the western-army brother running hot for the administration, the Valley brother voting Lincoln quietly inside a Democratic regiment. Same family, same war, different armies — and the archive preserves the difference in temperature exactly."),

dict(qid='a4-family-1', act=4, theme='family', source=('cand', 46), recip='to his mother', date_str='April 15, 1864',
  stem='Charles describes society ladies staging shows “for the benefit of the soldiers.” What organization were they funding?',
  choices=['The U.S. Sanitary Commission — civilian relief for the army’s sick and wounded', 'The Freedmen’s Bureau', 'The Grand Army of the Republic', 'The American Red Cross'],
  answer=0,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. The Sanitary Commission was the war's great civilian relief machine — inspecting camps, supplying hospitals, running relief boats — and it ran overwhelmingly on money women raised.",
  whyWrong={'3': "Clara Barton was at work in the war — but the American Red Cross wouldn't exist until 1881. The Sanitary Commission was its great predecessor.",
            '1': "The Freedmen's Bureau arrives in March 1865, a government agency for the formerly enslaved — different mission, different year."},
  expansion="The Sanitary Commission was what a nation without a welfare state improvised when its army started dying of preventable causes: a private organization, federally chartered in 1861, that inspected camps, shipped vegetables and bandages by the steamboat-load, ran hospital transports and soldiers' homes, and pioneered the data-driven public health the army itself lacked. Its fuel was female labor and ingenuity — seven thousand local aid societies, mostly women, feeding the depots; great Sanitary Fairs in the cities (Chicago's raised ~$100,000, New York's Metropolitan Fair over a million) turning charity into civic spectacle. Historians mark it as a hinge in American women's public life: managing supply chains and six-figure budgets built organizational muscle that suffrage and reform movements would inherit. Charles's 'tableaux, etc.' — amateur theatricals priced for a good cause — is the machine's smallest gear, turning in occupied New Orleans, thousand miles from the depots it fed.",
  context="The scene deserves a second look: 'the ladies HERE' means New Orleans — occupied, Confederate-hearted New Orleans — with the president of the effort being Mrs. Banks, wife of the occupying general. Union charity as loyalty theater in a captured city; Charles reports it to his mother with his usual level gaze, neither mocking nor moved. Why does this sit in the FAMILY column? Because the Commission was the home front's love made logistical — the institutional form of every box Frances wished she could send (and in Act V, when Alexander writes 'nearly starved,' her ache to send one is in this archive verbatim). The women of Champlain ran their own aid society stitch-for-stitch with these New Orleans tableaux. The war sorted the family's women no less than its men; the Commission is where the sorting shows."),

dict(qid='a4-camp-2', act=4, theme='camp', source=('cand', 52), recip='to his sister Fannie', date_str='June 21, 1864',
  stem='James inventories the pigs, chickens, and gardens of a Louisiana town — then notes foraging is “strictly forbidden.” What does the juxtaposition tell you?',
  choices=['His regiment was unusually well supplied', 'The temptation and the prohibition lived side by side — and discipline varied by army, campaign, and hour', 'Union policy always protected civilian property', 'He is complaining about officer corruption'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. A hungry column cataloguing livestock it is forbidden to touch — you can hear the appetite in the list. Whether the rule held depended on the colonel, the campaign, and the darkness.",
  whyWrong={'2': "'Always' is the trap — protection was the OFFICIAL posture in 1862 and in garrisoned Louisiana, but by late 1864 other Union armies were foraging by explicit order.",
            '0': "Supply had nothing to do with it — the list IS the longing. Well-fed soldiers didn't inventory hen roosts."},
  expansion="Union policy toward Southern property ran a four-year arc from protection to requisition to organized destruction, and a soldier's letters can be dated by where they sit on it. In 1862, Alexander's hen-roost raid (Act II) was a crime cheerfully committed under a conciliatory policy; by June 1864, James marches under commanders in the Gulf holding the 'strictly forbidden' line for occupied, nominally-reconstructing Louisiana; and that same autumn, Sheridan in the Shenandoah and Sherman in Georgia made foraging and barn-burning instruments of strategy — war on the enemy's economy, rations by order ('bummers' as a job title). Same army, same year, opposite rules, chosen by theater and purpose. The lesson generalizes: 'what was Union policy?' is usually the wrong question. Policy was a dial, and it turned with the war's logic — from wooing the South back, to starving its armies out.",
  context="Put the two foraging letters side by side — Alexander's hen roost (1862) and James's forbidden gardens (1864) — and the family gives you the war's moral arc in miniature, narrated by two different temperaments. Alexander confesses the theft with a comedian's flourish; James inventories the temptation and reports the rule, both faithfully, appetite and obedience in the same paragraph — the scholar's son to the end. He's writing to Fannie again (James's letters tilt toward his sister; the archive's People Web can show you the pattern), and there's something touching in what he thinks she'd want to know: the pigs, the gardens, the town's French-speaking strangeness. Four months from Cedar Creek, this is James still half-tourist. The Shenandoah — where his army will burn exactly the barns he's here forbidden to touch — is the dial's next stop, and his."),

dict(qid='a4-body-2', act=4, theme='body', source=('cand', 55), recip='to his mother', date_str='October 29, 1864',
  stem='Wounded at Cedar Creek, James “walked several miles after being hit in order not to fall into their hands.” What fate was he walking away from?',
  choices=['Summary execution — neither side took wounded prisoners', 'Capture, and a Confederate prison system that was killing men by the thousands', 'Being mistaken for a deserter', 'Amputation by Confederate surgeons'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. By October 1864 every Union soldier knew the names Andersonville, Belle Isle, Salisbury. A wounded man who could still move had one thought: stay off that ledger.",
  whyWrong={'3': "Confederate surgeons treated Union wounded much as their own — amputation odds were grim everywhere. The terror wasn't the enemy's hospital; it was his stockade.",
            '0': "Wounded prisoners were taken and generally not harmed at capture — the dying came later, slowly, inside the camps."},
  expansion="The prisoner-exchange cartel of 1862 had collapsed — wrecked above all on the Confederacy's refusal to exchange Black soldiers as soldiers — and by 1864 prisoners on both sides sat in camps built for fractions of their population. Andersonville held 33,000 men in twenty-six open acres that August; nearly 13,000 died there of scurvy, dysentery, and exposure — the war's single most lethal acre. Union soldiers knew: escaped and exchanged men's accounts filled the papers, and 'don't be taken' became field doctrine you can find in letters across every army. That is the calculus running in a twenty-year-old's mind at Cedar Creek — shot, in shock, doing miles on foot because the arithmetic said walking might kill him but the stockade would. It also explains the war's late bitterness like little else: by 1864, surrender no longer read as safety. James's sentence is that entire deterioration, written from a hospital bed with the same modesty he once used about sore legs.",
  context="This is the family's most quietly astonishing sentence, and James delivers it like a train schedule: hit at Cedar Creek — the dawn rout-turned-victory that saved Sheridan's Valley campaign and Lincoln's October — he chose miles of walking wounded over capture, then downplayed it to his mother from the hospital. (The next sentence in the letter, also in this archive: thirty miles in an army wagon to reach a railroad. The evacuation was its own campaign.) Remember June's James, sprawled on his tent floor after one day's march? October's James walks farther than that, shot. The family's memory kept its own mark on this battle: for 160 years they remembered his death the following autumn as October 19 — Cedar Creek's anniversary — when the record proves October 12. Grief filed him under this day. The archive corrected the date and kept the filing; both are true kinds of remembering, and this game carries the corrected one."),

dict(qid='a4-purse-2', act=4, theme='purse', source=('gap', 'IV-purse', 2), recip='to his mother', date_str='November 30, 1864',
  stem='Charles reports “seven months pay is now due us” — and that the single dollar his mother sent may be all he needs. What was army pay actually like by 1864?',
  choices=['Raised and paid promptly after the 1863 reforms', 'Chronically months in arrears — soldiers’ families floated THEM as often as the reverse', 'Paid weekly in camp by the sutler', 'Withheld until discharge by law'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Seven months without a paymaster — and the mother on a border farm mailing her soldier son a dollar. The war's cash flowed both directions, and the army's own ledger ran perpetually late.",
  whyWrong={'0': "Pay DID rise to $16 in mid-1864 — but a raise you haven't been handed for seven months is arithmetic, not income.",
            '3': "Only the held-back portion of the bounty waited for discharge. Wages were owed every two months — 'owed' being the operative word."},
  expansion="The paymaster's department was the Union's least reliable machine: pay was due every two months, but campaigning armies might not see the pay table for half a year — units in the 1864 campaigns commonly ran five to eight months in arrears, and the government owed its soldiers tens of millions at any given moment. The consequences cascaded homeward: families budgeted around remittances that didn't come, soldiers borrowed from sutlers at ruinous terms against pay that existed only on paper, and (as here) the flow could invert entirely — parents wiring dollars to privates. Note the date on Charles's shrug, too: November 30, 1864, deep in winter quarters after the Valley campaign, a man so far from anywhere to SPEND money that a mother's single dollar covers his needs. The soldier economy's last secret was exactly that: in the field, cash was mostly hope in paper form; the sutler, the express office, and the family kitchen were the real banks.",
  context="One sentence holds the whole family economy inverted: Frances — widow, farm, wartime prices — mailing a dollar south, and Charles gently closing the account: it 'will be all I will need, I think.' The tenderness is in the bookkeeping, as always with Charles; he won't let her send more, and he wraps the refusal in cash-flow logic she can't argue with. Compare Act I, where Henry budgets $26 to send HOME: three years of war have reversed the arrows without touching the principle — this family holds itself together by post, and money is one of its love languages. When the paymaster finally does appear (Act V holds Alexander expressing $350 home in one bolt, and still waiting on a LAST paymaster in his final letter), the arrears of 1864 are what that flood was repaying. The ledger always balanced eventually; the waiting was the war."),

dict(qid='a4-nation-2', act=4, theme='nation', source=('cand', 32), recip='to his mother', date_str='September 18, 1864',
  stem='Alexander is “glad to see the copper heads don’t like McClellan.” Who were the Copperheads?',
  choices=['Confederate cavalry raiders in the border states', 'Northern Peace Democrats who opposed the war — named for the snake by their enemies', 'Army contractors profiteering on supplies', 'Soldiers who deserted to the West'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Copperheads were the Union's internal opposition — Peace Democrats demanding an armistice — and soldiers loathed them with a special heat reserved for enemies who lived safely at home.",
  whyWrong={'0': "The name sounds like a raid, but the snake in question coiled in northern legislatures and newspapers, not the border thickets.",
            '2': "Contractors drew their own fury ('shoddy' entered English as a noun for their cloth) — but the Copperhead's offense was political: peace at the price of the Union."},
  expansion="The Peace Democrats — 'Copperheads' to their enemies, after the snake that strikes without warning — held that the war was a failed abolitionist crusade and demanded negotiation with the Confederacy. In August 1864 they captured the Democratic platform itself, declaring the war a 'failure' and nominating... George McClellan, the army's beloved ex-commander, who promptly repudiated the plank he ran on. That contortion is what delights Alexander here: if the Copperheads distrusted McClellan, then a soldier could read McClellan as not-quite-surrender — 'it speaks well for him,' runs the rest of his sentence's logic, even from a Lincoln man. Then September rewrote everything: Atlanta fell (Alexander's own army group took it), Sheridan swept the Valley (Winchester was fought the day after this letter, with James's army), and the 'failure' platform collapsed under the headlines. Soldiers' letters that autumn treat Copperheadism as the war's last enemy — and the November vote (78% for Lincoln) was the army's answer, delivered by mail.",
  context="Political-junkie Alexander, writing from Atlanta eighteen days after his army took it — he has personally invalidated the Democratic platform's central claim and knows it; the gladness in this sentence has a conqueror's ease under it. The Hubbell brothers' politics were not uniform (Charles's two-Lincoln-vote company sits opposite this question), but on Copperheads the ranks converged: whatever a soldier thought of emancipation or generals, the man at home calling his war a failure while he bled for it earned a category of contempt all its own — the fireside patriot's final form. Trace Alexander's arc across this game and the 1864 letters are where his voice becomes fully political: the teenager defending his enlistment now audits party platforms from a captured city. The war educated its soldiers in many things; citizenship was not the least of them."),

dict(qid='a4-family-2', act=4, theme='family', source=('gap', 'IV-family', 3), recip='to his mother', date_str='December 9, 1864',
  stem='James, recovering from his wound, talks his mother down: two weeks without word from Alexander is no cause for alarm. What is this letter managing?',
  choices=['A quarrel between the brothers', 'The family’s early-warning system — siblings triangulating each other’s safety through the mails, and calibrating Mother’s fear', 'A request for money gone astray', 'News that Alexander had been captured'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. The brothers tracked each other by letter-frequency like instruments — and part of the job was regulating the needle on their mother's dial. “I do not see why you should be” is James taking a turn at the family watch.",
  whyWrong={'3': "Alexander was fine — marching through Georgia with Sherman, beyond the reach of any post office, which was the whole innocent explanation.",
            '0': "No quarrel anywhere in it — the machinery here is care, running on postmarks."},
  expansion="Sherman's March to the Sea, mid-November to December 21, 1864, was designed to be out of touch: the army cut loose from its rail line and telegraph at Atlanta and vanished into Georgia — sixty thousand men off the map, by intention, for five weeks. The North literally did not know where its army was; Lincoln told a crowd he knew 'what hole he went in at, but not what hole he will come out of.' Every family with a son in that army ran the same silence Frances ran — and the Hubbells' case shows the period's coping machinery at its best: the family as a distributed sensor network, siblings cross-referencing dates ('he has only been absent two weeks'), damping each other's alarms, metering reassurance by post. James's arithmetic was exactly right, and its vindication was national news: Sherman surfaced at Savannah days after this letter and wired Lincoln the city as 'a Christmas gift.' Somewhere in that army, Alexander started a letter home.",
  context="Savor the role reversal: JAMES — shot at Cedar Creek seven weeks earlier, the brother the family should be fretting over — is the one steadying his mother about ALEXANDER, the untouchable veteran. 'You speak of being anxious about Alex. I do not see why you should be': it's the gentlest possible correction, a convalescent twenty-one-year-old doing emotional watch-standing from a hospital's reach. Every family under long fear develops these rotations — whoever is safest at the moment holds the mother's line — and the archive lets you watch the Hubbells trade the duty for four years. One more thing gives this letter its ache at a century and a half: of the two brothers in it, the one being worried about came home. James, the one soothing her, had ten months to live — not to a bullet, but to the journey home itself. Act V carries that."),
]
